 acts as a heat  dome trapping hot air underneath it it  can stay there for days or even weeks  sally lockwood is in athens and sent  this report  [Music]  the intense heat dome from the sahara is  wreaking havoc across north africa and  now europe  as it moved northwards into italy  temperatures rose to dangerous new highs  the island of sicily already in a state  of emergency  has recorded europe's hottest ever  temperature of nearly 49 degrees celsius  greece still battling some of the worst  wildfires in its history  is now braced for more destruction  amid fears the heat dome will head this  way  the fires came within a few miles of  athens while they've been brought under  control officials can do nothing about  the temperature in the city  heat stress is impacting people's health  their ability to go about their lives  and many of those things that we rely on  to cool us down like air conditioning  exacerbate climate change  the images we saw in all the  international media will i hope be a  wake-up call for the global community in  that here there is something happening  that will lead us to absolute  destruction  and flooding now in turkey has destroyed  buildings and claimed lives the heat  wave caused deadly wildfires and now  deadly deluge it's a domino effect  when the air heats up it holds more  water more water to be evaporated so  that increases drought but of course  that water has to go somewhere and that  increases the heavy rain events as well  many are worried flooding and landslides  will follow in greece too  just one of many countries feeling the  relentless impact of climate change  sally lockwood sky news athens  well earlier i spoke to eleni miravilli  athens first chief heat officer and only  the second in the world i asked her  why the city needs one the heat chief  officers started as a collaboration  between  two cities first was  miami-dade and then it was athens with  the  arts rockefeller resilience center  and  we put together the 